  "answer_text": "## What the verifier is complaining about\n\n`bpf_res_spin_lock`/`bpf_res_spin_unlock` are tracked by the verifier as a **stack of held locks**, and they must be released in strict **LIFO (reverse acquisition) order**. The error\n\n```\nbpf_res_spin_unlock cannot be out of order\n```\n\nmeans that on some explored path the verifier sees an unlock of a lock that is *not* the most-recently-acquired one.\n\n## Why it triggers here\n\nAcquisition order in the function is:\n\n1. `lock_a`\n2. `lock_b`\n\nSo the only legal release order is `lock_b` then `lock_a`.\n\nAt the bottom of the function you wrote:\n\n```c\nbpf_res_spin_unlock(&lock_a);   /* releases the OLDER lock first  -> wrong */\nbpf_res_spin_unlock(&lock_b);\n```\n\nBecause the unlock of `lock_a` is common to both the error path and the success path, the compiler hoisted it (insn 8\u201310 in the log) ahead of the `w6` branch check. On the path where `bpf_res_spin_lock(&lock_b)` succeeded, both locks are held, and the first unlock encountered is `lock_a` \u2014 i.e. releasing the older lock while the newer `lock_b` is still held. That is the out\u2011of\u2011order release the verifier rejects.\n\n## The fix\n\nRelease the locks in reverse order of acquisition (`lock_b` first, then `lock_a`):\n\n```c\n\tbpf_res_spin_unlock(&lock_b);\n\tbpf_res_spin_unlock(&lock_a);\n```\n\nFull corrected function:\n\n```c\nSEC(\"?tc\")\nint entry(struct __sk_buff *ctx)\n{\n\tif (bpf_res_spin_lock(&lock_a))\n\t\treturn 0;\n\tif (bpf_res_spin_lock(&lock_b)) {\n\t\tbpf_res_spin_unlock(&lock_a);\n\t\treturn 0;\n\t}\n\tbpf_res_spin_unlock(&lock_b);\n\tbpf_res_spin_unlock(&lock_a);\n\treturn 0;\n}\n```\n\nThe error path is already correct: if `bpf_res_spin_lock(&lock_b)` fails, only `lock_a` is held, so unlocking just `lock_a` is the proper top-of-stack release. With the swap, every path releases locks in strict LIFO order and the verifier is satisfied.",
